.TH WTOP 1 "2026-09-29" "wtop @PROJECT_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wtop \- a Windows Task Manager style top for the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wtop
[\fB\-d\fR \fIms\fR]
[\fB\-t\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-s\fR [\fIW\fBx\fIH\fR]]
.br
.B wtop
\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-v\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wtop
is an interactive system monitor modelled on the
.I Performance
page of the Windows Task Manager: the \(lqw\(rq stands for the Windows
Task Manager look and \(lqtop\(rq for the classic Unix command.
.PP
Each resource has its own screen (tab) with a 60\-second history graph
and a statistics panel:
.TP
.B CPU
Utilization, current and base speed, processes, threads, load average,
up time, sockets, cores, logical processors and cache sizes.
.TP
.B Memory
Memory usage graph, composition bar (in use, modified, standby, free),
available, committed, cached, slab, buffers, shared memory and swap.
.TP
.B Disk N
Active time and transfer rate graphs, average response time, read and
write speed, capacity, formatted size, system disk, swap and mount points.
.TP
.B Network
Receive and send throughput, link speed, IPv4 and IPv6 addresses, MAC
address, Wi\-Fi signal strength and total bytes transferred.
.TP
.B GPU N
Utilization and dedicated memory graphs, temperature, clock speed,
power, video encoder and decoder load, driver and PCI location.
.PP
A tab line at the top lists every screen, a tmux\-style status line and an
htop\-style function key bar sit at the bottom, and on wide terminals a
sidebar shows every resource with a live mini graph.
.PP
.B wtop
runs on Linux only.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-delay\fR \fIms\fR
Refresh interval in milliseconds. Default 1000, minimum 100.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-tab\fR \fIn\fR
Open tab number \fIn\fR first (0 is CPU, 1 is Memory, ...).
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-snapshot\fR [\fIW\fBx\fIH\fR]
Print every tab once as plain text and exit. The size defaults to the
terminal size, or 120x40 when the output is not a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a usage summary and exit.
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version and license and exit.
.SH INTERACTIVE COMMANDS
Function keys work like in
.BR htop (1):
.TP
.B F1
Show or hide the key binding help.
.TP
.B F10
Quit.
.PP
Tabs are switched with
.BR tmux (1)\-style
bindings: press the prefix \fBCtrl+b\fR, release it, then press a command
key. The status line shows \fB^B\fR while the prefix is pending.
.TP
.B C\-b n\fR, \fBC\-b p
Next, previous tab.
.TP
.B C\-b Left\fR, \fBC\-b Right
Previous, next tab.
.TP
.B C\-b 0\fR ... \fB9
Go to the tab with that number.
.TP
.B C\-b l
Go back to the last used tab.
.TP
.B C\-b w
Choose a tab from a list; move with \fBj\fR/\fBk\fR or the arrow keys,
select with \fBEnter\fR, close with \fBEsc\fR.
.TP
.B C\-b ?
Show the key binding help.
.TP
.B C\-b d\fR, \fBq\fR, \fBCtrl+c
Quit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/stat\fR, \fI/proc/cpuinfo\fR, \fI/proc/loadavg\fR, \fI/proc/uptime
CPU statistics.
.TP
.I /proc/meminfo
Memory statistics.
.TP
.I /proc/diskstats\fR, \fI/sys/block\fR, \fI/proc/mounts\fR, \fI/proc/swaps
Disk statistics and layout.
.TP
.I /proc/net/dev\fR, \fI/sys/class/net\fR, \fI/proc/net/wireless
Network statistics.
.TP
.I /sys/class/drm
AMD and Intel GPU statistics.
.TP
.I /usr/share/hwdata/pci.ids\fR, \fI/usr/share/misc/pci.ids
Optional PCI name database for readable adapter and GPU names.
.SH NOTES
NVIDIA GPUs are read through
.BR nvidia\-smi (1),
which must be on the \fBPATH\fR; it is queried on a background thread.
.PP
The terminal needs 24\-bit color and UTF\-8 support; graphs are drawn with
braille characters. The minimum size is 60x20.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 when standard input or output is not a terminal, 2 on
invalid command line arguments.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B wtop \-d 500
Refresh twice per second.
.TP
.B wtop \-t 1
Start on the Memory tab.
.TP
.B wtop \-\-snapshot 120x40 > report.txt
Save a plain text report of every screen.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR top (1),
.BR htop (1),
.BR tmux (1),
.BR proc (5)
.SH AUTHOR
Tom J.
.SH LICENSE
MIT.
